<BaseLayout title="Techy Case Study · Santiago Madriz" description="A public-safe engineering case study for Techy, a real commerce product in Costa Rica.">
  <header class="page-intro" data-reveal><div class="eyebrow">Case study · Modernization in progress</div><h1>Techy: engineering a real commerce experience.</h1><p>A private product repository with a deliberately public-safe technical story. No customer data, credentials, private endpoints, or sensitive operational details are disclosed here.</p></header>
  <article class="prose" data-reveal>
    <h2>Context</h2><p>Techy presents Apple products for customers in Costa Rica and supports direct purchase conversations. Unlike a portfolio demo, it has real brand, content, catalog, legal, and operational constraints.</p>
    <h2>Current architecture</h2><p>The inherited application uses Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, and Sanity. Its App Router structure includes catalog, product detail, open-box, contact, purchasing guidance, legal pages, robots metadata, and a generated sitemap.</p>
    <blockquote>The objective is not to expose a private business repository. It is to demonstrate how a real product is understood, protected, tested, and improved.</blockquote>
    <h2>Modernization priorities</h2>
    <h3>Baseline and ownership</h3><p>Preserve history, separate the new private origin from the former maintainer’s upstream repository, document configuration, and establish a reproducible build before product changes.</p>
    <h3>Quality strategy</h3><p>Add unit coverage around pricing and query boundaries, integration coverage for content behavior, and Playwright journeys for catalog discovery, product detail, legal navigation, and WhatsApp handoff.</p>
    <h3>Production readiness</h3><p>Introduce pull-request checks, dependency review, observable failures, stable environment documentation, and explicit deployment ownership.</p>
    <h2>Constraints and tradeoffs</h2><p>The repository remains private because business configuration and future operational logic should not be exposed for portfolio value. Public documentation will describe decisions and outcomes without reproducing commercially sensitive implementation details.</p>
    <h2>Next measured outcome</h2><p>The first milestone is a green, reproducible build with a documented environment contract and automated critical-path smoke coverage. Performance and conversion work should follow a measured baseline rather than assumptions.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
